Name the steps between the last startup phase and bsd_init

The startup phase finishes and the kernel does not reach bsd_init:
the last startup landmark is on the screen and bsd_init's own first
landmark is not.  The stretch between them is a list of calls in
kernel_bootstrap_initialize_upto(), and most of them are already
announced by kernel_bootstrap_log() with the name of the step.

They are not on the screen because kernel_bootstrap_log() only prints
when STARTUP_DEBUG_VERBOSE is set.  It prints now whenever the console
is there, which is what the flag was standing in the way of.

The steps that had no name get one, so that the last name on the
screen is the step the boot reached.

---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index c0de4bd..e84b8a2 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -223,8 +223,7 @@ static inline void
 kernel_bootstrap_log(const char *message)
 {
 	boot_progress_stage(message);
-	if ((startup_debug & STARTUP_DEBUG_VERBOSE) &&
-	    startup_phase >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
+	if (startup_phase >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
 		kprintf("kernel_bootstrap: %s\n", message);
 	}
 	kernel_debug_string_early(message);
@@ -716,6 +715,7 @@ kernel_bootstrap_thread(void)
 	 *	Initialize the shared region module.
 	 */
 	vm_commpage_init();
+	kernel_bootstrap_log("commpage");
 	vm_commpage_text_init();
 
 #if CONFIG_MACF
@@ -785,6 +785,7 @@ kernel_bootstrap_thread(void)
 
 #if KPERF
 	kperf_init_early();
+	kernel_bootstrap_log("kperf");
 #endif
 
 	/*
